<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <PackageId>Utos.Workflow.Validation</PackageId>
    <RootNamespace>Utos.Workflows.V1.Validation</RootNamespace>
    <Description>Validation rules for Utos workflow bundles, as specified by api/docs/workflow-validation.md and the static expression rules of api/docs/template-expressions.md. Reports coded, path-addressed issues so the CLI, the daemon and every tool enforce one rule set. Depends on Utos.Workflow and on Acornima, the ECMAScript parser the expression grammar is defined over.</Description>
    <PackageTags>utos;workflow;validation;protobuf</PackageTags>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Depends on Utos.Workflow and on Acornima, and deliberately on nothing else. A validation
    framework forced on every consumer of a spec package would be an imposition that cannot be
    walked back without a breaking change, and its flattened message strings would not survive
    the trip to another language anyway. Callers that want FluentValidation can adapt
    ValidationReport in a few lines.

    Acornima is the first exception, and the expression rules require it: a grammar over ECMAScript
    syntax trees needs an ECMAScript parser, and this is the one the reference engine (Jint) parses
    with, so the tree validated here is the tree that runs.

    JsonSchema.Net is the second, and the schema rules require it for exactly one rule. UTOS-H008 —
    a `default` validating against the schema that declares it — is the only rule here that
    evaluates a schema rather than inspecting one, and the schema is not known until a bundle is
    read, so nothing generate-ahead can serve it. Every other UTOS-H0## rule is a structural walk
    over the Struct and uses no evaluator at all.

    Both clear the same bar the CLI sets: AOT- and trim-compatible, because the NativeAOT `utos`
    binary links this assembly. For JsonSchema.Net that holds only if schemas are parsed with
    JsonSchema.FromText — JsonSerializer.Deserialize<JsonSchema> is the reflective overload and
    raises IL2026/IL3050, verified with `dotnet publish -p:PublishAot=true` on a probe consuming
    this package.

    The AOT analyzer enforces that, and does so from here rather than only at the CLI's publish.
    It is enabled tree-wide in Directory.Build.props, which it could not be while these packages
    targeted netstandard2.0 — the analyzer does not run there (NETSDK1210), so the one check that
    would have caught a reflective JsonSchema parse was unavailable on the assembly the NativeAOT
    binary links.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Utos.Workflow\Utos.Workflow.csproj" />
    <PackageReference Include="Acornima" />
    <PackageReference Include="JsonSchema.Net" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- The splitter and the grammar are tested below the public entry point. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Utos.Workflow.Validation.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- All source here is hand-written; EnableDefaultCompileItems=false (see Directory.Build.props). -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="**\*.cs" Exclude="bin\**\*.cs;obj\**\*.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\..\CHANGELOG.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="docs\" Visible="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
